 #edb/ref 1031 "The Division Algorithm" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050451 #edb/ref 1033 2689 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050451 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "49978329-baa9-545d-916c-f0072c761ab2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050884 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048637 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050884 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050697 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050884 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152086 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050884 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152087 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050884 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152088 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050884 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152089 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050884 #edb/ref 1031 "Universal and Existential Quantifiers" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050884 #edb/ref 1033 2787 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050884 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6de9a5a5-ab7e-5d26-821c-9f50ad7ae54d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048536 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048904 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049187 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049272 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049533 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049908 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050794 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050890 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051023 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054834 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054835 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054836 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1031 "Factoring Linear Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1033 87 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051085 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7f29909c-8f45-5a17-820a-13a0a327cd5f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161726 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14981" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161726 #edb/ref 1071 "Fill in the blanks so that the following reasoning is correct:\n\nThe notation $3 \\mid 18$ means ![](images/06/06b772f72fe6d6f1ee02bdc961f6438561083573e0e0d9d261c7594cd48e86bc.svg) It is equivalent to the statement that there exists \n![](images/c4/c4ebc4da7c8037d0ca6a752df8a4dc3933918db39ac972e4a9b5068fac834140.svg) such that ![](images/88/88e94f9f45ba18035aaea40fb665ecd374c878f9ac7c44aeb038f2d210c02a28.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161726 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ada57336-8fdb-584d-b03c-7b7e31cbf55a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161726 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a \\mid b$ means $a$ divides $b.$ So, $3 \\mid 18$ means ![](images/5a/5aaf3d46d0bf568ead42806495080fa6eab9d567aa33d22faeee925233867b0a.svg)\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=a k.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3 \\mid 18$ is equivalent to the statement that there exists $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{an integer}}$ $k$ such that $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}18 = 3k}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161727 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14982" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161727 #edb/ref 1071 "Fill in the blanks so that the following reasoning is correct:\n\nThe notation $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means ![](images/fb/fb6fbc91b2218218acab5a0fe1baed797fc7ece0800c3280bc0d53af6d765ae3.svg) It is equivalent to the statement that for $\\[6pt]$ ![](images/48/48442365817dc8d3a14cc2f5931284b07e8cbfccf0bd2a097f7362f0f97348cb.svg)\n![](images/20/20865eee0320e3c8d4822421723a5a251048e7158888ac6416427f632bcb85b7.svg) we have ![](images/45/45453872ffd37b70f166312cf1aa4c3966437e91dc293ac0db198d895e41821c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161727 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1df938d2-db9c-5335-9f54-e44c40659992" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161727 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a \\not\\mid b$ means $a$ does not divide $b.$ So, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means ![](images/83/833ccbc866a4951df8fa798f0780922c3dc56bff0b2baf1a36711c3f47c9f313.svg)\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb \\neq a k.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ is equivalent to the statement that for $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{ any}}$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{integer}}$ $k,$ we have $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 \\neq 5k}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161728 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14983" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161728 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $4 \\mid (6x+9y)$\n\n- $5 \\mid (6x+9y)$\n\n- $3 \\mid (6x+9y)$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161728 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b06423e-99a9-5ac2-a413-93aa71ac4fbe" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161728 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a \\mid b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=a k.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $6(0)+9(1) = 9,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $6(1)+9(0) = 6,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x+9y = 3(2x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3 \\mid (6x+9y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161923 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050884 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161923 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162471 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161923 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162472 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161923 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407526 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161923 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407534 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161923 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407535 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161923 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407536 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161923 #edb/ref 1039 "Stating the Definition of Integer Divisibility" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161923 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161726 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161923 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d0e960a4-872c-55c0-8439-60426671f5cf" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161924 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049558 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162469 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162473 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407525 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407527 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407528 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407529 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407533 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161924 #edb/ref 1039 "Stating the Definition of Non-Divisibility" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161924 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161727 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161924 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "05d3b242-3ecd-54d6-a763-925e2baada2c" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161925 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051085 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162470 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162474 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407530 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407531 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407532 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407537 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161925 #edb/ref 1039 "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161925 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161728 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186161925 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162740 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162741 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162742 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162743 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162213 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162744 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162213 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162213 #edb/ref 1045 "Integer Divisibility" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162213 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162740 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162213 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048610 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162213 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162213 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9610f5eb-4c2a-5356-ab60-5e12da9a7560" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162470 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407517 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162470 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221882" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162470 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162470 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $6∣(42x+24y)$\n- $7∣(42x+24y)$\n- $2∣(42x+24y)$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162470 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f097a3f6-b1f4-531f-833b-43edfbbc8726" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162470 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=6(7x+4y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $7x+4y$ must be an integer, too. So, $6∣(42x+24y).$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $42(0)+24(1)=24,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=2(21x+12y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $21x+12y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2∣(42x+24y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407488 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407491 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407497 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162471 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221899" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162471 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162471 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162471 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1062095b-125d-5701-81d9-4b57bb308f4f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162471 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$ So, $2∣8$ means $2\\text{ divides }8.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $2∣8$ is equivalent to the statement that there exists $\\text{an integer}$ $k$ such that $8=2k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162740 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162741 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162740 #edb/ref 1061 162161 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162740 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b0fa360f-9e02-5a70-b17f-90340c91003c" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162740 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163373 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162741 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162744 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162741 #edb/ref 1061 162162 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3902c14b-dd71-5c37-bd20-4fb2177d36a2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161923 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162742 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162743 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162742 #edb/ref 1061 162166 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3ae5e0bc-75a2-5dd9-b814-d4bdb58d123f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161924 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162743 #edb/ref 1061 162169 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162743 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d795623f-e963-5223-922d-0146dbda23bb" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162743 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161925 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162744 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162742 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162744 #edb/ref 1061 162181 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162744 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7cc84b4a-0d80-572a-b21b-ca0aa54f80ff" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186162744 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163374 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163373 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $a$ and $b$ are integers where $a\\neq 0.$ We say that $\\boldsymbol a$ **divides** $\\boldsymbol b$ and write \n\n$$\na\\mid b\n$$\n\nif there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\nb = ka.\n$$\n\nWe can write this more formally as follows:\n\n$$\na\\mid b \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\exists k \\in \\mathbb Z, b = ka\n$$\n\nFor example, $2\\mid 8$ because there exists an integer ${\\color{blue}{k}} = {\\color{blue}{4}}$ such that\n\n$$\n8 = {\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot 2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163373 #edb/ref 1065 9351 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163373 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "100e1a89-de92-5d1a-86ba-bcd72190ae6d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163373 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163374 #edb/ref 1064 "We have the following definition of integer divisibility. \n\n$$\na\\mid b \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\exists k \\in \\mathbb Z, b = ka\n$$\n\nIf $a$ **does not divide** $b,$ we write $a\\not\\mid b.$ Therefore, by negating the two statements above, we get the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(a∣b)\\, & ⇔\\,¬(∃k\\in Z,b=ka) \\\\ a∤b\\, & ⇔\\,∀k\\in Z,b\\ne ka\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $a\\not\\mid b$ means that $b \\neq ak$ *for all* integers $k.$\n\nFor example, $3\\not \\mid 8$ because there does not exist an integer ${\\color{red}{k_2}}$ such that\n\n$$\n8 = {\\color{red}{k_2}}\\cdot 3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163374 #edb/ref 1065 9355 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163374 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1a06989c-367e-5d20-840c-e5ab34c82911" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186163374 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Divisibility" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407370 #edb/ref 1081 "8=16k" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407370 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407370 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "0341c354-0b34-5253-835d-01bf5f74e9d3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407371 #edb/ref 1081 "an integer" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407371 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407371 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "046494f7-4180-59bc-84aa-9b1496ae4ecb" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407372 #edb/ref 1081 "integer" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407372 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407372 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "0494a0c4-3cbc-5304-8920-956b6740222b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407373 #edb/ref 1081 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407373 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407373 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "070efb65-293c-5069-a098-e98293361bfb" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407374 #edb/ref 1081 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407374 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407374 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "077fe